    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d740">

                <pb facs="0842" n="730" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg730"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.20/542</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 3633</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-06-20">June 20, 1935</date>.<lb/>[Received July
                        13.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to enclose for the
                    Department’s information a copy of despatch No. 32 of May 31, 1935, from the
                    Consulate General at Canton<anchor n="73" xml:id="d740-f1"/> giving such
                    information as it has been able to obtain in regard to the recently established
                    airplane factory at Shiuchow, Kwangtung.</p>
                <p>The despatch indicates that there will be a substantial market for American
                    aviation materials if the present plans for the factory materialize, and, in
                    connection with this matter, mentions the possible desirability of the American
                    Government’s revising its present attitude towards the enforcement of the
                    regulations governing the exportation of American aviation materials to China.
                    With reference to the Legation’s attitude towards this question, the
                    Department’s attention is again invited to the Legation’s despatch No. 3291 of
                    January 23, 1935, in which the present unsatisfactory situation from the point
                    of view of American business is described.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson Trusler
                    Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
